reation; adding a new one at class body is fine.\n\nlogger.py: extend formatter. Need `_truncate_message` helper, and `max_message_chars` keyword-only param.\n\nCurrent format:\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\nNeed to truncate message before formatting, without mutating record. Approach: compute the message text: `record.getMessage()` gives message after args substitution. Then truncate it. Then need to format with truncated message but keeping exc_info/stack_info. Approach: use a copy of the record via `copy.copy(record)` and set `msg` to truncated and args to (). Use `logging.makeLogRecord`? Simpler:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated is not message:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nBut careful: the truncation of a copy: original record untouched. However the `record.message` attribute — `super().format` sets `record.message = record.getMessage()`. Since it's a copy, fine.\n\nBut `_truncate_message` must first do `%s` substitution — `record.getMessage()` does that. But if the message contains no args... fine.\n\nAlso: if truncated is not message — I return original message object when no truncation, so identity check works. Better return None to signal no truncation. I'll return the truncated string or the same object.\n\nWait, subtlety: truncating may be needed even when message length <= budget → return original message; identity check fine.\n\nMulti-line prefix logic uses `record.message` — after copy, `record.message` is truncated message. `msg.find(record.message)` works.\n\nHmm but if truncation makes the message contain \"...<omitted>...\" marker plus newline possibilities — fine.\n\nAlso we must ensure exc_info/stack_info preserved in copy — copy.copy copies attributes including exc_info, stack_info. Good.\n\nNote: When record.message was truncated, `msg.find(record.message)` — the formatted message contains record.message. Yes.\n\n`keep_full_message` must be exactly True: `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True` — but careful with `1`: `1 is not True` → True, so truncation applied. Good.\n\nValidation:\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None) -> None:\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be None or an int >= 32, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        ...\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\nHmm, should non-int like \"64\" raise ValueError — yes per spec \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". So the check covers.\n\nTruncate helper:\n```python\n_TRUNCATION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker = _TRUNCATION_MARKER\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nBudget 32: remaining = 32-15 = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches spec.\n\nIf remaining <= 0... max_chars >= 32 and marker len 15, so remaining >= 17. Fine.\n\n_setup_logger: pass `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX